+++
title: Dura-Europos
description: Ancient Syrian city
key-facts:
  - name: When it was built
    value: c. 300 BC
    from: "built: c. 300 BC"
  - name: When it was abandoned
    value: 256–257 AD
    from: "abandoned: 256–257 AD"
  - name: Where it is
    value: near Salhiyah, Syria
    from: "location: near Salhiyah, Syria"
  - name: Cultures that lived there
    value: Hellenistic, Parthian, Roman
    from: "cultures: Hellenistic, Parthian, Roman"
  - name: When it was excavated
    value: 1922–1937 1986–present
    from: "excavation-dates: 1922–1937 1986–present"
  - name: What state it is in
    value: Partially destroyed and looted by the Islamic State
    from: "condition: Partially destroyed and looted by the Islamic State"
image-plan:
  - file: DuraEuropos-WadiSouth.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The southern wadi and part of the city walls. Dura-Europos was built
      on an [[ escarpment ]] 90 metres (300 feet) above the southwestern bank
      of the [[ Euphrates ]].
  - file: DuraEuropos-PalmyraGate.jpg
    role: full
    section: history
    caption: The Palmyrene Gate, the principal entrance to the city. It was
      photographed by [[ John Henry Haynes ]] in 1885, when the American
      "Wolfe Expedition" rediscovered the site.[[ ^89 ]]
  - file: Dura-Europos_plan.png
    role: full
    section: rebuilding-of-the-city
    caption: The general excavations plan. Rectangular blocks defined by
      cross-streets ranged round a large central [[ agora ]] — the [[
      Hippodamian ]] model the city was rebuilt on in the 2nd century BC.[[ ^9
      ]]
  - file: Dura_Europos_synagogue_destruction.svg
    role: inline
    section: siege-and-destruction
    caption: "How the synagogue came to be buried: successive steps of backfilling
      of the Wall street in Dura-Europos and the subsequent destruction of the
      synagogue. After Kraeling, 1956."
  - file: Ceiling_Tile_with_Heliodoros,_an_Actuarius.jpg
    role: inline
    section: inhabitants-languages-and-texts
    caption: A tile from the ceiling of a House of Scribes, its Greek inscription
      naming the man Heliodoros and his occupation, [[ actuarius ]]. The
      frontal pose, large eyes, subtle shading and earth-toned pigments recall
      other painted decoration in the city's buildings.[[ ^36 ]]
  - file: Julius_Terentius_Performing_a_Sacrifice_(Dura_Europos).jpg
    role: full
    section: inhabitants-languages-and-texts
    caption: Julius Terentius performing a sacrifice. Commissioned by the Roman
      tribune at Dura-Europos, who is depicted and identified here by Latin
      inscription, the painting mixes Greek, Roman, and Palmyrene elements,
      perhaps as a diplomatic gesture.
  - file: Large_Altar_to_Gods_of_Palmyra_1.jpg
    role: inline
    section: inhabitants-languages-and-texts
    caption: "The altar of [[ Yarhibol ]]: a man with a Latin name, Scribonius
      Moucianus, holding a Greek-titled office, [[ chiliarch ]], in the Roman
      army, dedicating in Greek to a local god.[[ ^50 ]]"
  - file: Dura_europos_artemis_louvre.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: Bust of a woman, probably [[ Artemis ]], from the Temple of Artemis.
      Only the upper part was found, both arms having been broken off.[[ ^54
      ]]
  - file: Dura_Europos_fresco_Sacrifice_of_Conon.jpg
    role: full
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: The sacrifice of Konon, a wall painting from the [[ Temple of Bel |
      temple-of-bel-dura-europos ]], which was once richly decorated with
      them.
  - file: DuraEuropos-TempleOfBel.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: The Temple of Bel, built in one corner of the city wall in the third
      century BC.
  - file: Relief_of_Atargatis_and_Hadad_from_Dura-Europos.tif
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: "The relief from the [[ Temple of Atargatis ]]: the goddess with a
      lion on either side of her, her husband [[ Hadad ]] nearby, and a cult
      standard between the two deities.[[ ^60 ]]"
  - file: Thymiaterion_Green-Glazed_Altar.jpg
    role: drop
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: "[[ Thymiaterion ]] (incense altar) from the Cistern at the Temple of
      Atargatis. It depicts a group of deer, Atargatis sacred animals.[[ ^59
      ]]"
  - file: Ag-obj-25182-001-pub-large.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: "The female protective deity of Palmyra, from the [[ Temple of the
      Gadde ]], in a guise modelled on the [[ Tyche ]] of [[ Antioch ]]:
      seated between two figures, wearing a [[ mural crown ]] and Greek
      clothing."
  - file: Ag-obj-6832-001-pub-large.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: "The god [[ Arsu ]] on a camel, from the [[ Temple of Adonis |
      temple-of-adonis-dura-europos ]]. The inscription under the figure
      reads: \"Oga the sculptor has made (this to) 'Arsu the camel-rider', for
      the life of his son\".[[ ^67 ]]"
  - file: Ag-obj-24942-001-pub-large.jpg
    role: drop
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: Relief with the figure of Iarhibol, from the [[ Dolicheneum ]].
  - file: Life-sized_male_head_in_polos_(Zeus_Megistos^)_-_YDEA_-_25259.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: A head, perhaps of Zeus Megistos, whose temple on the Acropolis
      probably replaced a Greek temple of Zeus Olympius.
  - file: Ag-obj-6833-003-pub-large.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: The stele from the [[ Temple of Zeus Kyrios ]], erected by Baratheh,
      son of Luke and his son Abubuhi in 31 AD. Its [[ Palmyrene language ]]
      inscription names the god as [[ Baalshamin ]]; the Greek one names him
      as Zeus.[[ ^72 ]]
  - file: Relief_of_Aphrodite_in_a_niche.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: Relief of [[ Aphrodite ]] in a niche, dated to 200–256 AD. It was
      found in a private house close to the military quarter that may have
      been a brothel.[[ ^74 ]]
  - file: Votive_Relief_of_the_goddess_Nemesis.jpg
    role: inline
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: Marble relief of the goddess [[ Nemesis ]] receiving a burnt offering
      by a priest named Julius Aurelius Malochus, with a wheel and a griffin
      at her feet.[[ ^74 ]]
  - file: Nike_or_Winged_Goddess_of_Victory.jpg
    role: drop
    section: greco-roman-palmyrene-and-local-cults
    caption: Nike or Winged Goddess of Victory.
  - file: Dura_Synagogue_WC3_David_anointed_by_Samuel.jpg
    role: full
    section: synagogue
    caption: David anointed by Samuel, from the assembly hall of the synagogue —
      the largest cycle of paintings that has survived from antiquity.[[ ^80
      ]]
  - file: Dura_Europos_fresco_Jews_cross_Red_Sea.jpg
    role: inline
    section: synagogue
    caption: "[[ Israelites ]] [[ crossing the Red Sea ]]. The synagogue paintings
      are preserved at [[ Damascus ]]."
  - file: Baptistery_wall_painting_Procession_of_women.jpg
    role: full
    section: house-church
    caption: Procession of women, from the [[ baptistry ]] of the house church.
      Dating from 235 AD, its frescoes are probably the most ancient Christian
      paintings in existence.[[ ^82 ]]
  - file: Cumont_and_Rostovtzeff_in_the_Mithraeum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: mithraeum
    caption: Cumont and Rostovtzeff in the Mithraeum, unearthed in January 1934
      after years of expectation as to whether Dura would reveal traces of the
      Roman [[ Mithraic | mithraism ]] cult.
  - file: Ag-obj-6891-001-pub-large.jpg
    role: inline
    section: mithraeum
    caption: Relief from the Mithraeum, which stood between towers 23 and 24 and
      is now on display at the [[ Yale University Art Gallery ]].
  - file: Aerial_view_of_Dura_in_late_1930s.jpg
    role: full
    section: timeline-of-known-events-at-dura-europos
    caption: Aerial view of Dura in late 1930s. Yale University Art Gallery,
      negative no. 2184
  - file: A08_Dura_Europos_615.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: View of excavations and the [[ Euphrates ]].
  - file: Aphrodite_with_turtle-AO_20126-IMG_4347-gradient.jpg
    role: inline
    section: palace-of-the-strategos
    caption: Statue of Aphrodite with a turtle, found in the city.
  - file: Graffito_of_Clibanarius.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-finds
    caption: Graffito showing a warrior on a horse in full armor.
  - file: Horse_Armor_from_Dura-Europos.jpg
    role: full
    section: archaeological-finds
    caption: Horse armor. Each set was made of nearly 2,000 individual overlapping
      thin, scale-like plates bound together with wire; these are the only
      full sets of scale horse armor ever found.[[ ^94 ]][[ ^95 ]]
  - file: Sasanian_helmet.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-finds
    caption: Sasanian helmet.
  - file: Ag-obj-5959-001-pub-large.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-finds
    caption: "The [[ Scutum from Dura-Europos ]], 3rd century AD: a rectangular
      arched shield measuring 105.5 by 41 centimetres (41.5 by 16.1 in), found
      broken into thirteen pieces at Tower 19.[[ ^96 ]]"
  - file: Wooden_Shield_with_Scenes_from_the_Iliad.jpg
    role: full
    section: archaeological-finds
    caption: One of three painted wooden shields with scenes of the Trojan War,
      found one right a top of the other in January 1935. Painting by [[
      Herbert J. Gute ]].[[ ^97 ]]
  - file: Looting_of_Dura-Europos,_2011-2015,_image_by_DigitalGlobe.png
    role: full
    section: looting-by-isis
    caption: Looting of Dura-Europos, 2011–2015, satellite images by
      DigitalGlobe.[[ ^102 ]]
further:
  - slug: palmyra
    why: Traces of close ties with Palmyra are part of why Dura-Europos is no
      longer read as a great caravan city; Palmyrene traders, soldiers, clans
      and gods are all over the site — and unlike Palmyra, Dura left no
      funerary portraits at all.
  - slug: antioch
    why: Dura controlled the river crossing on the route between Seleucus's newly
      founded cities of Antioch and Seleucia on the Tigris — and the Palmyrene
      goddess in the Temple of the Gadde was modelled on the Tyche of Antioch.
  - slug: mari-syria
    why: The nearby ancient city that Dura-Europos was listed alongside when
      UNESCO's "Tentative List" was revisited in 2011.
